==Latin==

===Etymology===
Related to {{term|atrox||cruel, fierce, frightful|lang=la}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|la}} {{recons|atro-ek|lang=ine-pro}}, from  {{etyl|ine-pro|la}} {{recons|h₁eh₂ter-|lang=ine-pro}}, {{recons|eh₂ter-||fire|lang=ine-pro}}, whence Sanskrit {{term|अथर्वन्|tr=átharvan|lang=sa}} and Avestan {{term|𐬁𐬙𐬀𐬭𐬱||fire|tr=ātarš|lang=ae}}, see also Umbrian {{term||...|tr=atru|lang=xum}}, Oscan {{term||...|tr=Aadíriis|lang=osc}}, Irish {{term|áith||kiln|lang=ga}}.
 {{rfscript|Ital}}<!-- Umbrian and Oscan, above -->, + {{recons|h₃okʷ-||lang=ine-pro}}, {{recons|h₃ekʷ-||eye; to see|lang=ine-pro}} (compare Latin {{term|oculus|lang=la}}, Lithuanian {{term|akìs|lang=lt}}, {{etyl|cu|-}} {{term|око||tr=oko|lang=cu}}, Albanian {{term|sy|lang=sq}}, {{etyl|grc|-}} {{term|ὤψ||tr=ōps|eye, face|lang=grc}}, {{etyl|hy|-}} {{term|ակն||tr=akn|lang=hy}}, Avestan {{term|||tr=aši|eyes|lang=ae}}, Sanskrit {{term|अक्षि||tr=ákṣi|lang=sa}}, Tocharian A {{term|ak|lang=xta}}).  Influenced by {{term|ater|āter|black, dark|lang=la}}, by analogy with {{term|ferox|ferōx|wild|lang=la}}.

